## 1) Severity: MEDIUM | Type: Test
Title: Concurrency test is racy and may not detect or reliably reproduce the failure

Affected:
- tests/base/src/test/java/org/keycloak/tests/admin/group/GroupTest.java:136-158

Explanation:
The reader thread is started but never joined. The main thread sets `deletedAll` and asserts immediately, so an exception thrown by an in-flight request after the assertion is missed. The thread can also outlive the test and keep calling the admin client after realm cleanup, which can cause flaky failures in later tests. `deletedAll` is also never set if a delete throws, so the loop would run forever. Finally, the reader may not have run a single iteration before deletion starts, so the test may pass without exercising the race. Fix: keep a reference to the thread, set the flag in a `finally` block, join the thread, then assert. Optionally use a latch so the reader has done at least one read before deletion starts.

Proposed fix:
~~~java
Thread reader = new Thread(...);
reader.start();
try {
    groupUuuids.forEach(id -> managedRealm.admin().groups().group(id).remove());
} finally {
    deletedAll.set(true);
    reader.join(10000);
}
assertThat(caughtExceptions, Matchers.empty());
~~~

## 2) Severity: LOW | Type: Bug
Title: getSubGroupsCount returns null when the group was concurrently deleted

Affected:
- model/infinispan/src/main/java/org/keycloak/models/cache/infinispan/GroupAdapter.java:274-275

Explanation:
The `Long` return value is now null if the underlying model has disappeared. Callers that unbox it, such as `GroupUtils` or the representation builders, may hit an NPE instead. The fix only moves the failure and depends on every caller handling null. Check the callers, or return 0 or throw a consistent not-found exception. This is a risk only if callers are outside the diff and unboxing.

Proposed fix:
~~~java
GroupModel model = modelSupplier.get();
return model == null ? 0L : model.getSubGroupsCount();
~~~
